<%# AN EXISTING RULE THAT NO LONGER MATCHES THE SPENDING. Local: `suggestion`.

    §8's copy target: `Groceries has averaged $470 for 4 periods, your rule says $420`. This and
    the dead rule are the reason §8 is a permanent page rather than a wizard — drift is how a
    budget quietly stops being true, and a one-time setup can never catch it.

    BOTH FIGURES ARE PER PERIOD, and neither is `budgets.amount`. The rule's side is
    `Budget#steady_ask` — a $260-a-month rule under a biweekly user claims $120 a period — so the
    two numbers in this sentence are in the same unit and can honestly be subtracted. The amount
    the ACCEPT link carries is a different figure again: the engine inverts `steady_ask` back into
    the rule's own column unit before putting it on the wire, and the edit form labels it there.

    DRIFT TO ZERO IS THE STARKEST SENTENCE ON THE PANEL and it renders like any other: an envelope
    a category feeds, four complete periods, and $0.00 observed. %>
<p>
  <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= suggestion.detail[:pool_name] %></span> has averaged
  <span class="tabular-nums"><%= number_to_currency(suggestion.detail[:observed]) %></span> a period for
  <%= pluralize(suggestion.detail[:periods], "period") %>, and your rule asks for
  <span class="tabular-nums"><%= number_to_currency(suggestion.detail[:rule_amount]) %></span> a period.
</p>

<p class="text-gray-500">
  <% gap = (suggestion.detail[:observed] - suggestion.detail[:rule_amount]).abs %>
  <% if suggestion.detail[:direction] == :up %>
    You are spending <%= number_to_currency(gap) %> a period more than the rule reserves — the difference
    is coming out of something else.
  <% else %>
    The rule is reserving <%= number_to_currency(gap) %> a period more than you spend — that money is
    sitting in the envelope instead of funding anything else.
  <% end %>
</p>
